BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Gardens": "https://gardens.duke.edu/event/botany-spotlight-meet-the-meat-eaters-carnivorous-plants/"}
X-SOURCE-ID:gardens
X-SOURCE:Sarah P. Duke Gardens
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261014T130000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261014T153000
DTSTAMP:20260706T122226Z
CREATED:20260706T122226Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20260706T122226Z
UID:17466-1791982800-1791991800@gardens.duke.edu
SUMMARY:Botany Spotlight: Meet the Meat Eaters—Carnivorous Plants
DESCRIPTION:Enter the remarkable world of carnivorous plants with Alec Motten\, professor emeritus\, Duke University Department of Biology. Start in the classroom with an overview of the impressive array of structures that make carnivory possible—from digestive glands and sticky traps to slippery pitfalls\, trap doors and snap-shut jaws. Look closely at these structures under a microscope\, then venture outside to meet specimens living in the garden. Whether you’re captivated by the iconic Venus flytrap’s sudden snap\, the drowning pools and pit-fall traps of pitcher plants or the elegant patience of a sundew’s glistening tentacles\, this program will forever change the way you think about what plants are capable of. Pair this class with a tour of the Duke Research Greenhouse’s teaching collection\, which includes an impressive collection of carnivorous plants\, on Saturday\, November 21 (register separately). \nLocation: Pelham-Cullen Classroom inside the Barnes Welcome Center. \nFee: $28\, 20% discount for Duke Gardens members with discount code. \nParking: Parking fees apply. \nAccessibility: This will be a small group program that takes place partially seated indoors and partially outdoors\, traveling on a combination of paved and unpaved paths with some uneven surfaces\, moderate inclines and stairs. The indoor location is equipped with a hearing loop\, which transmits sound directly to hearing aids and cochlear implants with a T-coil setting. Accessible restrooms are available onsite.
URL:https://gardens.duke.edu/event/botany-spotlight-meet-the-meat-eaters-carnivorous-plants/
LOCATION:Duke Gardens–Pelham-Cullen Classroom in the Barnes Welcome Center\, 420 Anderson Street\, Durham\, NC\, 27705\, United States
CATEGORIES:Adult Programs
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://gardens.duke.edu/wp-content/uploads/BG_bog-white-topped-pitcher-plant-sarracenia-leucophylla-Bodine-13.jpg
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Calendar 1 Ics": "https://calendar.unc.edu/event/engagement-in-research-101"}
X-SOURCE-ID:calendar_1_ics
X-SOURCE:UNC Chapel Hill
DTSTAMP:20260913T043502Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_53693403952371
DTSTART:20261014T140000Z
DTEND:20261014T160000Z
DESCRIPTION:Engagement in Research 101: Introduction to Engaging Patient & 
 Community Partners in Research\n\n \n\nAre you interested in learning abou
 t patient and community engagement and how it can benefit your research? E
 xcited by the idea of engaging patient\, community\, or other partners in 
 your research\, but unsure about where to start? Want to know more about h
 ow to engage partners at different points throughout your study?\n\n \n\nE
 ngaging with patient and community partners who are impacted by your resea
 rch can be instrumental to the success of your study. This online training
  will provide an overview of engagement in research\, highlighting that en
 gagement is not "all or nothing" or "one-size-fits-all"\, but instead enco
 mpasses a wide variety of low touch to high touch approaches that could be
  right for your study.\n\n \n\nThe session will cover common myths/misconc
 eptions about engagement in research\, benefits of and key considerations 
 for engaging patient and community partners in research\, and initial step
 s/existing resources you can leverage to begin engaging partners in your r
 esearch.\n\n \n\nRegister\n\n \n\nPresenters:\nAlicia Bilheimer\, MPH - Di
 rector of Engaged Science\, NC TraCS\nVeronica Carlisle\, MPH\, CHES - Sen
 ior Community Health Educator\, UNC Lineberger Comprehensive Cancer Center
  (LCCC)\nNisha Datta\, MS - Senior Project Manager\, NC TraCS\nSimone Fran
 k\, MPH - Senior Project Manager\, NC TraCS\nJennifer Potter\, MPH\, CHES 
 - Senior Program Coordinator for Clinical Outreach\, LCCC\n\n \n\nEngaging
  Patient\, Community\, and Other Partners in Your Research is a multi-part
  online training series. You may register for the entire series OR any sin
 gle training session. This training series was developed collaboratively w
 ith patient\, community\, and researcher partners and is co-sponsored by t
 he UNC Lineberger Comprehensive Cancer Center and NC TraCS Institute.
LOCATION:
SUMMARY:Engagement in Research 101
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.unc.edu/event/engagement-in-research-101
CATEGORIES:Workshops and Classes
END:VEVENT
