X-SOURCE:NC Cultural Resources
DTSTAMP:20260913T043432Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_53178468717233
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20270501
DESCRIPTION:An article published in the April 30\, 1819 edition of the Rale
 igh Register and North Carolina Gazette proclaimed the following: “It is
  not probably known to many of our readers\, that the citizens of Mecklenb
 urg County\, in this State\, made a Declaration of Independence more than 
 a year before Congress made theirs. The following Document on the subject 
 has lately come to the hands of the Editor from unquestionable authority\,
   and is published that it may go down to posterity.”  \n\nThe document 
 in question was called the Mecklenburg Declaration of Independence\, and i
 t had allegedly declared Mecklenburg “free and independent” from Briti
 sh rule on May 20\, 1775. Of the 27 men who were said to have signed the d
 ocument\, almost all were Presbyterian.\n\nBut many people were confused 
 – why were they just now hearing about this revolutionary action? The or
 iginal document burned in a house fire in 1800\, and the published version
  had been reconstructed from notes made by the clerk of the 1775 meeting. 
 No extant copies could be found.\n\nThe debate over the authenticity of th
 e Mecklenburg Declaration began. And\, when a copy of a document called th
 e Mecklenburg Resolves written on May 30\, 1775\, was discovered in a 1775
  newspaper\, the debate intensified. The Resolves were revolutionary\, but
  stopped short of declaring independence. Did both documents exist? Or was
  the Declaration being conflated with the Resolves?\n\nExamine the evidenc
 e and decide for yourself by visiting the new exhibit\, “Mecklenburg Dec
 laration?” at the Presbyterian Heritage Center.\n\nThe PHC is open Tuesd
 ay-Friday (10-4) and Saturday (12-4).
GEO:35.646737;-82.300463
LOCATION:Presbyterian Heritage Center
SUMMARY:Mecklenburg's Declaration: NC's Revolutionary Presbyterians
URL;VALUE=URI:https://events.dncr.nc.gov/event/mecklenburgs-declaration-ncs
 -revolutionary-presbyterians
CATEGORIES:Exhibits & Tours
CATEGORIES:Exhibitions
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Calendar 1 Ics": "https://calendar.unc.edu/event/to-beethoven-rather-than-to-seem-part-v-gut-instinct-chamber-music-project"}
X-SOURCE-ID:calendar_1_ics
X-SOURCE:UNC Chapel Hill
DTSTAMP:20260913T043504Z
UID:tag:localist.com\,2008:EventInstance_53886589929874
DTSTART:20270501T233000Z
DTEND:20270502T003000Z
DESCRIPTION:FEATURING\n\nNicholas DiEguenio\, violin\nMimi Solomon\, piano\
 n\n \n\nPROGRAM\n\nFeaturing Beethoven’s “Spring” Sonata and Robert 
 Honstein’s Olmsted\n\nThis season\, Gut Instinct takes on the large-scal
 e project “To Beethoven Rather Than To Seem\,” marking 200 years since
  Beethoven’s death and 250 since the U.S. Declaration of Independence. O
 ver the course of five concerts\, Nicholas DiEugenio and pianist Mimi Solo
 mon will present all ten Beethoven Sonatas on period instruments along wit
 h five commissioned works written expressly for them.\n\n \n\nPARKING\n\nP
 lease plan to arrive early as parking on campus can be tricky. Learn more 
 about parking and directions to UNC-Chapel Hill’s campus.\n\n \n\nThe Gu
 t Instinct Chamber Music Project\, now in its third season\, is a concert 
 series in the Triangle area which brings UNC-Chapel Hill and Duke Universi
 ty’s historical instrument collections together with artists committed t
 o presenting inspired\, visceral\, historically informed performances of t
 he 19th-century chamber music repertoire. The series features music of fem
 ale composers and their artistic circles. The name “Gut Instinct” is a
  play on words. In 19th-century Europe\, the violin\, viola\, and cello we
 re strung with (sheep) gut.\n\n \n\nAll of the artists collaborating with 
 the Pleyel and Graf pianos at UNC-Chapel Hill (built in 19th-century Paris
  and Vienna\, respectively)\, as well as the Clementi piano at Duke (early
  19th-century England) use these gut string materials. The turn of phrase 
 “gut instinct” also refers to the visceral\, accessible nature of the 
 musical performances that this series presents. Our mission is to make “
 Historically Informed Performance” accessible\, relatable\, and cultural
 ly relevant! We also want to feature these special pianos that are rarely 
 heard by the public\, along with great music by female composers which has
  remained lesser known to the greater public for far too long\, and living
  composers who are keeping our art form alive!
GEO:35.912507;-79.052471
LOCATION:Person Hall
SUMMARY:“To BEethoven Rather Than To Seem”: Part V | Gut Instinct Chamb
 er Music Project
URL;VALUE=URI:https://calendar.unc.edu/event/to-beethoven-rather-than-to-se
 em-part-v-gut-instinct-chamber-music-project
CATEGORIES:Performances
END:VEVENT
